+## Activate skills
+
+A catalog skill enters the system prompt only when it is active. Active skills come from:
+
+- Session/CLI: `--skill <path>` (repeatable, additive even with `--no-skills`) or `/skills add <name> --session`
+- Global: `~/.pi/agent/skills.json`
+- Repository: `~/.pi/agent/repo-skills.json`, keyed by git repository identity
+- Directory: `~/.pi/agent/directory-skills.json`, keyed by the current checkout or worktree root, or by the working directory outside a git repository
+
+On a name collision, the first skill wins. Persisted scopes load in global, repository, then directory order. Explicit `--skill` files and interactive session paths are passed to the loader as additional paths, so they do not override a same-name persisted skill. The same skill file enabled in several scopes loads once.
+
+Package- and settings-provided skills that previously loaded automatically must now be enabled in `~/.pi/agent/skills.json` or the applicable entry in `~/.pi/agent/repo-skills.json` or `~/.pi/agent/directory-skills.json`. Use `pi skills list` to find catalog skill names.
+
+Enable global skills with `~/.pi/agent/skills.json`:
+
+```json
+{
+  "enabled": [
+    "~/.agents/skills/code-review-and-quality",
+    { "source": "~/.agents/skills/browser-testing-with-devtools" }
+  ]
+}
+```
+
+Enable repository-scoped skills with `~/.pi/agent/repo-skills.json`:
+
+```json
+{
+  "github.com:earendil-works/pi": {
+    "enabled": ["~/.agents/skills/pi-development"]
+  },
+  "local:~/Documents/repos/private-tool": {
+    "enabled": ["~/.agents/skills/private-tool"]
+  }
+}
+```
+
+Repository keys are resolved from git remotes in this order: `upstream`, then `origin`, then the first configured remote. Remote URLs are normalized so `git@github.com:earendil-works/pi.git`, `https://github.com/earendil-works/pi`, and `ssh://git@github.com/earendil-works/pi.git` all become `github.com:earendil-works/pi`. Non-default ports stay part of the key. If no remote exists, Pi falls back to `local:<repo-root-relative-to-$HOME>`, such as `local:~/Documents/repos/private-tool`.
+
+Enable skills for one directory with `~/.pi/agent/directory-skills.json`:
+
+```json
+{
+  "/Users/me/Documents/repos/pi-worktrees/feature": {
+    "enabled": ["./.agents/skills/feature-notes"]
+  },
+  "/Users/me/scratch": {
+    "enabled": ["~/.agents/skills/scratchpad"]
+  }
+}
+```
+
+Clones and worktrees share repository-scoped entries only if they resolve to the same repository key. For example, a fork that selects its own `origin` instead of the original repository's remote gets a different key. Directory scope follows the path instead: inside a git repository the key is the root of the current checkout or worktree, as `git rev-parse --show-toplevel` reports it, so each worktree has its own entries; outside a repository the key is the working directory itself. Keys are real paths, so a directory reached through a symlink shares the entries of its target. Removing a directory's last entry removes its key; entries for directories that no longer exist are left in place and have no effect.
+
+Manage activation with either the interactive `/skills` command or the one-shot CLI:
+
+```text
+/skills active
+/skills list
+/skills search browser
+/skills add browser-testing-with-devtools --session
+/skills add browser-testing-with-devtools --repo
+/skills add browser-testing-with-devtools --directory
+/skills remove browser-testing-with-devtools --global
+/skills reload
+
+pi skills active
+pi skills list
+pi skills search browser
+pi skills add browser-testing-with-devtools --global
+pi skills add browser-testing-with-devtools --repo
+pi skills add browser-testing-with-devtools --directory
+pi skills remove browser-testing-with-devtools --repo
+```
+
+`--session` is interactive-only and lasts until the session ends. `--global`, `--repo`, and `--directory` persist to their respective JSON configuration. `list` and `search` show catalog skills. `pi skills active` shows the configured global, repository, and directory entries; `/skills active` also shows session entries.
+
+Path arguments may use `~`, `$NAME`, or `${NAME}`; `/skills` expands them itself because its arguments never pass through a shell. With `--repo`, a path inside the current checkout is stored relative to the repository root, so the entry works in every clone and worktree with the same remote. With `--directory`, a path inside the directory base is stored relative to that base. Otherwise `add` keeps a `~` or `$NAME` spelling as typed and stores any other path as an absolute path, under `~` when it is in your home directory. Stored paths are expanded whenever Pi loads skills: at startup, after `/skills add` or `/skills remove`, and on `/reload` or `/skills reload`. Other relative entries resolve against the repository root in `repo-skills.json`, against the directory base in `directory-skills.json`, and against the working directory in `skills.json`, so prefer absolute, `~`, or `$NAME` paths in `skills.json`.
+
+Use `--no-skills` to suppress global, repository, and directory active skills; explicit `--skill` paths still load.
